    "title": "NDAA passing could mean improvements at Sioux Gateway - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Reilly Mahon Maren Ewertz ,\n1 hours ago ( KCAU ) — After being promised federal defense funding for runway improvements at Sioux Gateway Airport years ago, there is new hope that millions of dollars could finally be approved for that work.\nThe project is included in the National Defense Authorization Act ( NDAA ) for 2026, which was passed on Wednesday, December 10, by the U.S. House of Representatives.\nThe legislation still needs approval in the Senate as well as from President Trump.\nAccording to Iowa Representative Randy Feenstra’s office, funding for runway repair and replacement, aircraft parking apron, and a warm-up/holding pad at the Sioux Gateway are included in the funding package.\nStory continues below\nRepresentative Feenstra said the funding from the NDAA would help protect almost 1,000 jobs, along with continuing to promote economic growth in the Siouxland region.\nThis comes after the full Iowa Congressional Delegation sent a last in November to the armed services committee, in both chambers requesting funding.\nThe improvements are critical to keeping the local air base operational. KC-135 Stratotankers currently take off from Sioux Gateway below capacity because of runway length and weight restrictions.\nWithout improvements, there is a likelihood that the Refueling Squadron would be relocated. Iowa Senator Joni Ernst and northwest Iowa Representative Randy Feenstra are joining city and business leaders, along with the 185th campaigning for project funding in both Washington as well as at the Sioux City base.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to SiouxlandProud | Sioux City, IA | News, Weather, and Sports.",
